<urn:atm:triage:finding/AG-ARCH-GATE-DESIGN-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AG-ARCH-GATE-DESIGN-001" ;
  triage:title "atm-architecture hard-gate crate (first draft) reproduces the same failure patterns it was built to stop" ;
  triage:description "Following the 2026-07-19 verdict that the AG.18-AG.25 codex-orchestration ladder run was a failure, arch-ctm began designing structural (compiled) enforcement in a new crates/atm-architecture crate: delete-list manifests per sprint (ag18/20/22/24/25.toml), boundary_enforcement.rs, and scripts/check-ag-sprint-gates.sh. This work was first found uncommitted directly on develop (see relocation instruction sent to arch-ctm; not itself a finding, but the trigger for this review). quality-mgr ran an independent stray background review of the gate design itself (review-only, no changes) and reported 7 issues, several of which are not new mistakes but literal recurrences of failure modes already catalogued elsewhere in this same phase's triage set:\n\n1. CRITICAL: manifests (ag18/20/22/24/25.toml) cite single-file paths (peer_transport.rs, tests/runtime_root.rs) already split into module directories by AG.18-23; scan_manifest_file() has no existence check and will hard-panic on every real ladder branch, not just fail cleanly. This is the same stale-path-citation defect pattern as ATM-QA-AG24-003 (sprint doc citing dead runtime_health.rs:589-600 line numbers) and quality-mgr's own AG24/AG25-LOC-GATE notes that arch-ctm's code citations (composition.rs:1160, tests/runtime_root.rs:335/472) do not correspond to real content on those branches.\n2. Gate scans all 8 sprints' manifests globally against whichever branch is checked out, not the active sprint's own diff -- README admits it is 'intentionally failing today,' i.e. designed to be permanently red. Not a usable binary CI gate as shipped.\n3. Not wired into any GitHub workflow yet (zero grep matches) -- currently local-script-only, advisory despite being called a 'merge gate.'\n4. Literal/regex denylist is trivially defeated by rename -- this exact evasion (e.g. classify_io_error -> classify_error) has already been DOCUMENTED 4 TIMES this ladder as the repeatable RBQA-F006/007/008 (+ this, F009-adjacent) field-presence-classification leak. A rename-defeatable denylist does not close the class of defect it targets.\n5. No requirement that a sprint's diff actually touch the file containing the forbidden symbol -- a sprint could hit its LOC threshold via unrelated deletions and pass clean. This is structurally the same substitution risk as item 7 below and as the original HARD-MERGE-GATE-LOC finding's ladder-wide-vs-per-sprint measurement ambiguity.\n6. ATM_ARCH_ALLOW_GATE_CHANGES=1 unlocks edits to the gate's own rule files with no audit trail or paired ADR/.ttl requirement -- self-certifying compliance risk (the gate can loosen itself with no record, mirroring how sprint docs' own Hard Merge Gate prose could be read either way without a discovered violation forcing the issue).\n7. base/head refs are caller-supplied with no sanity check against git merge-base -- the same ladder-wide-vs-per-sprint substitution trick already used once (per quality-mgr) to close the original LOC-gate finding without truly closing it. HARD-MERGE-GATE-LOC, AG24-LOC-GATE, and AG25-LOC-GATE are the three prior occurrences that trick enabled or nearly enabled.\n\nSignificance for the phase-AG post-mortem: the ladder's core problem was never a one-off implementation slip -- it was that prose-based sprint-doc gates (LOC deltas, deletion targets, boundary rules) were unenforceable by review alone, and the same defect classes (stale citations, rename-evasion, branch-scope substitution) recurred 3-4+ times despite QA catching each instance individually. The first draft of the compiled replacement gate reproduces at least 3 of those same defect classes (items 1, 4, 7 above) rather than closing them, which is direct evidence that the fix must be reviewed with the same rigor as the ladder it replaces, not merged on the strength of being 'the corrective response.'" ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AG" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "arch-gate-design" ;
